On your lab VM (RHEL, Rocky, or Alma with systemd), create the app directory and a minimal server: sudo mkdir -p /opt/hello && printf 'from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer
class H(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    def do_GET(self):
        self.send_response(200); self.end_headers(); self.wfile.write(b"hello from systemd")
HTTPServer(("0.0.0.0", 8080), H).serve_forever()
' | sudo tee /opt/hello/app.py

notes.body:
A production script has a shape: shebang, 'set -euo pipefail', constants at the top, small 'local'-using functions, a main() that sequences them, logging with timestamps, and meaningful exit codes. Here is a disk-and-service health check in that shape: '#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
LOG=/var/log/healthcheck.log
log() { echo "$(date '+%F %T') $*" | tee -a "$LOG"; }
check_disk() { local use; use=$(df --output=pcent / | tail -1 | tr -dc 0-9); if [ "$use" -gt 85 ]; then log "FAIL disk ${use}%"; return 1; fi; log "OK disk ${use}%"; }
check_ssh() { if systemctl is-active --quiet sshd; then log "OK sshd"; else log "FAIL sshd"; return 1; fi }
main() { local rc=0; check_disk || rc=1; check_ssh || rc=1; exit $rc; }
main "$@"'. Worked example: walk the failure path when disk is at 91 percent. Solution: check_disk computes use=91, the -gt 85 test is true, log writes '2026-10-07 17:30:00 FAIL disk 91%' to both terminal (tee) and the log file, returns 1; main records rc=1 via '||' without tripping 'set -e' (failures in || lists are exempt); check_ssh still runs; main exits 1. The caller -- cron, Jenkins, a human -- sees exit 1 and the log explains exactly which check failed.
